  '''
}

docs {
  `recordsimilarity.v1.RecordSimilarity/Import` creates records that are not stored yet, as many as the caller sends,
  over one call. It is a stream both ways: the caller sends one message for each record and gets one answer for each,
  in the order they were sent. It is how a catalog is loaded.
  
  It only creates. A record whose `id` is already stored is left exactly as it is and answered
  `IMPORT_OUTCOME_EXISTS`: nothing is compared, nothing is replaced, and no anchor moves. Changing a stored record is
  an `Evaluate`, which judges the change. So a stream can be sent twice, or cut off and sent again from the start.
  
  ## This request
  
  Three messages. In Bruno, send each message and then end the stream.
  
  1. `bruno-import-1`: a pouch of gummies of the brand `brand-1`, with all four fields and two quantities.
  2. `bruno-import-2`: a vape cartridge, with an `anchor`: the record as it was first created, when its name did not
     say `Live Resin` yet. Drift is measured from that version.
  3. `bruno-import-3`: a record with a field `strain`, which the schema does not define.
  
  On an empty server:
  
  ```json
  { "id": "bruno-import-1", "outcome": "IMPORT_OUTCOME_CREATED" }
  { "index": "1", "id": "bruno-import-2", "outcome": "IMPORT_OUTCOME_CREATED" }
  {
    "index": "2",
    "id": "bruno-import-3",
    "outcome": "IMPORT_OUTCOME_FAILED",
    "status_code": 3,
    "message": "invalid record: unknown field `strain`; the schema has: brand, category, description, name"
  }
  ```
  
  The third record fails by itself and the stream goes on. The first answer's `index` is 0, which JSON leaves out.
  Sent a second time, the first two are answered `IMPORT_OUTCOME_EXISTS` and the third fails as before.
  
  `FindSimilar`, `Match`, and their examples search among what this creates.
  
  ## Request: a stream of `ImportRequest`
  
  | Field | Type | Required | Values |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `record` | `Record` | yes | The record as it is now. Its `id` is required here. |
  | `anchor` | `Original` | no | The record as it was first created, if that is still known: `fields` and `quantities`, with no `id` and no `brand_id`. Drift is then measured from this version and not from the record as it is now, and a field or quantity the record has since lost is remembered. Left out, the record as it is now is its own point of reference, as for any new record. |
  
  ### `record` (`Record`)
  
  | Field | Type | Required | Values |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `id` | string | yes | The caller's identifier for the record: any string that is not blank. A record without one cannot be stored and is answered `IMPORT_OUTCOME_FAILED`. |
  | `brand_id` | string | no | The key of the brand that owns the record. It is an identifier, compared exactly and never embedded: the brand's name, as text, is the `brand` field. Not blank. Stored with the record, as for `Evaluate`. If the id is already stored under another brand, or under a brand and this names none, the record is answered `IMPORT_OUTCOME_FAILED` with status 9. |
  | `fields` | map of string to string | yes | Field name to text. The names are the ones `Describe` lists: with the example config `name` (required), `category`, `brand`, and `description`. A field that is left out and one that is blank are the same thing: no value. Runs of whitespace count as one space. Text beyond the model's limit (256 tokens with the example config) is not read. |
  | `quantities` | map of string to `Quantity` | no | Quantity name to amount. The names are the ones `Describe` lists: with the example config `weight_per_item`, `volume_per_item`, `thc_per_item`, and `cbd_per_item`. A quantity the record does not have is left out. An amount is never sent as text in `fields`. |
  
  ### `anchor` (`Original`)
  
  | Field | Type | Required | Values |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `fields` | map of string to string | yes | As `record.fields`. The anchor has to fit the schema as a record does, so with the example config it needs a `name`. |
  | `quantities` | map of string to `Quantity` | no | As `record.quantities`. |
  
  A fault in the anchor is reported with `the original version:` in front of the message.
  
  ### A quantity (`Quantity`)
  
  A measured amount with its unit: a weight, a volume, a potency. A single value is a range whose two ends are equal,
  and a caller that has only one end of a range sends it as both.
  
  | Field | Type | Values |
  |---|---|---|
  | `min` | double | The low end. A finite number, 0 or more. |
  | `max` | double | The high end. A finite number, not below `min`. |
  | `unit` | string | The unit both ends are in. Not blank. Matched without regard to case or the space around it: `G` and `g` are one unit. |
  
  These units are converted before amounts are compared, so `1 g` and `1000 mg` are the same quantity:
  
  | Measures | Compared in | Spellings |
  |---|---|---|
  | a mass | `mg` | `mcg` `µg` `μg` `microgram` `micrograms`, `mg` `milligram` `milligrams`, `g` `gram` `grams`, `kg` `kilogram` `kilograms`, `oz` `ounce` `ounces`, `lb` `lbs` `pound` `pounds` |
  | a volume | `ml` | `ml` `milliliter` `milliliters` `millilitre` `millilitres`, `cl` `centiliter` `centiliters`, `l` `liter` `liters` `litre` `litres`, `fl oz` `floz` `fl. oz` `fl. oz.` `fluid ounce` `fluid ounces` |
  | a share of the whole | `%` | `%` `percent` `pct` |
  
  Any other unit (`pack`, `each`, a misspelling) is accepted and never an error. It is comparable with itself and with
  nothing else. `Quantities` > `ListUnits` gives the table the server runs with, each unit with its factor.
  
  ## Response: a stream of `ImportResponse`
  
  One for each record, in the order they were sent.
  
  | Field | Type | Meaning |
  |---|---|---|
  | `index` | uint64 (a string in JSON) | Which record of the stream this answers, counting from 0. |
  | `id` | string, may be absent | The record's `id` as it was sent. Absent if the record had none. |
  | `outcome` | `ImportOutcome` | One of the values below. |
  | `status_code` | uint32 | For `IMPORT_OUTCOME_FAILED`, the gRPC status code the same record would have got from `Evaluate`. 0 otherwise. |
  | `message` | string | For `IMPORT_OUTCOME_FAILED`, what was wrong, as a sentence. Empty otherwise. |
  
  | `ImportOutcome` | Number | Meaning |
  |---|---|---|
  | `IMPORT_OUTCOME_UNSPECIFIED` | 0 | Not sent by this server. |
  | `IMPORT_OUTCOME_CREATED` | 1 | Nothing was stored under the id. The record is now, at revision 1. |
  | `IMPORT_OUTCOME_EXISTS` | 2 | A record is already stored under the id. It was left exactly as it was, and the record sent was not embedded. |
  | `IMPORT_OUTCOME_FAILED` | 3 | The record was not stored. `status_code` and `message` say why. |
  
  | `status_code` | Status | When |
  |---|---|---|
  | 3 | `INVALID_ARGUMENT` | The record, or its anchor, does not fit the schema, or it has no `id`. |
  | 8 | `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED` | The model was busy, and the server waited ten seconds for room. |
  | 9 | `FAILED_PRECONDITION` | The id is stored under a brand the record does not name. |
  | 10 | `ABORTED` | Another writer kept changing the record. |
  | 14 | `UNAVAILABLE` | The vector store cannot be reached. |
  
  ## Errors
  
  Everything about a record is in its answer, and one record failing does not end the stream. An error on the call
  itself is the transport's.
  
  The stream shares the server's model with every other request, a few records at a time
  (`server.import_batch_records`, 8 in the example config). While the model is busy the stream is slowed down, not
  refused.
}
